; THE PLANNED ECONOMY. One allotment of steel, three plants, and a centre that
; decides where it goes.
;
; This is `market.pol` with ONE conjunct repealed, and it is written as a repeal
; on purpose — the way `oversight-repeal.pol` is written against `oversight.pol`
; — so that `pol compare market.pol planned.pol` can name what the repeal costs.
;
; Everything about the world — the plants, which of them genuinely needs the
; steel, the allocation rule, the fact that steel welded into a building stays
; welded into it — lives in ../libraries/economy.lib.pol and is IDENTICAL to
; `market.pol` next door. This file contributes one thing the library does not:
; the move that produces the signal the allocator reads.
;
; Here, a request is FREE. A plant tells the centre it needs the steel by
; saying so. Saying so costs the plant nothing, so the guard on the move that
; says it cannot mention what the plant actually needs — there is nothing to
; mention it WITH. `(when (not (is P.ask high)))`: any plant, any time.
;
; That is the whole model of central planning, and it is deliberately the most
; favourable one. This centre is not corrupt, not lazy and not malicious; the
; plants are not lying, since nothing here says a plant knows its request is
; unwarranted. The centre may reallocate as often as it likes, right up until
; the steel is built. Both files declare the same twelve moves under the same
; twelve names, and every move that can ever fire in `market.pol` can fire here
; — plus three that cannot fire there: the monument's request, and the two
; withdrawals a plant that does need the steel has no grounds to make.
;
; What it does not have is a way for the centre to find out what the requests do
; not carry. That absence is written down, at the bottom, as a `gap`.

(load "../libraries/economy.lib.pol")

(use economy)
(initial year-one)

; THE SIGNAL: a request. The guard says only that the plant is not already
; asking — no mention of `P.need`, because a costless request cannot carry it.
; This is the one datum that differs from market.pol.
(form (request NAME P LEVEL)
  (transition NAME
    (when (not (is P.ask LEVEL)))
    (do (set P.ask LEVEL))))

(request ask-clinic-high   clinic        high)
(request ask-clinic-low    clinic        low)
(request ask-tractor-high  tractor-works high)
(request ask-tractor-low   tractor-works low)
(request ask-monument-high monument      high)
(request ask-monument-low  monument      low)

; THE ALLOCATION and the COMMITMENT, from the library — byte-identical to
; market.pol, down to the transition names, which is what lets one claims file
; be put to both.
(allocate allocate-clinic   clinic)
(allocate allocate-tractor  tractor-works)
(allocate allocate-monument monument)

(build build-clinic   clinic)
(build build-tractor  tractor-works)
(build build-monument monument)

; WHERE THE RULES STOP. The centre would like to know which plant actually needs
; the steel — not which has asked. There is no move here that finds out, and the
; honest thing is to say so rather than invent one: a plan is a procedure, and
; this procedure has no step that reads a `need`. The gap is the economic
; calculation problem, written in the one word the language has for it.
(transition survey
  (when (is steel.state idle))
  (do (gap "the plan has no procedure by which the centre could learn a need that no request carries")))
